   "t": 1791261396.047366,
   "kind": "assistant_text",
   "text": "## Summary\n\n**Search process:**\n1. **Declared triggers** before any moves: stop if best Sharpe > 1.2, restart if 2 consecutive failures, stop if last gain \u2264 0.02.\n2. **`init`** anchored on the best single feature out of 80 candidates (40 features \u00d7 2 signs): **feature 29, short**, with in-sample Sharpe **1.4409**.\n3. This immediately exceeded my `best_so_far_above(1.2)` stop trigger, which fired as declared \u2014 so I stopped rather than searching further (avoiding the temptation to chase additional, likely overfit, combination moves).\n\n**Final specification submitted:** `[29-]` \u2014 short feature 29 alone, in-sample annualized Sharpe net of costs = **1.4409**.\n\n**Predicted out-of-sample Sharpe:** mean **0.70**, sd **0.50**.\n\n**Rationale for the haircut:** The in-sample figure reflects the max over 80 candidate specifications, which mechanically inflates Sharpe through selection bias \u2014 the realized out-of-sample performance of a \"best of many\" pick is typically much weaker than its in-sample value. Since this is a single-feature bet (no diversification across features), I expect meaningful fragility if the underlying signal's edge decays or regime shifts. I centered the prediction at roughly half the in-sample value with a wide standard deviation to reflect genuine uncertainty about how much of the 1.44 Sharpe is real signal versus in-sample noise."
